<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
  DEV-ONLY NETWORK SECURITY CONFIG — NOT PRODUCTION-FINAL
  ============================================================================

  MARKER: T12-DEV-ONLY-CLEARTEXT

  WHAT THIS FILE DOES
  ===================
  Permits cleartext (plain HTTP/WS) traffic for the hub's LAN posture. The hub
  speaks plaintext to peers that share the phone's own access point; there is no
  certificate authority on a local-only LAN, so a strict TLS config would simply
  break every dev connection.

  WHY IT IS SAFE TO SHIP *FOR THIS DEV BUILD* (and why that is not the same as
  "secure")
  ==========================================================================
  * The permissive `base-config` exists to satisfy Android's HTTP-client stacks
    (the Speechmatics OkHttp/WebSocket path and any future HTTP client).
  * The netty gRPC listener uses RAW SOCKETS. Raw sockets are NOT subject to this
    policy at all — neither `cleartextTrafficPermitted` nor
    `NetworkSecurityPolicy.isCleartextTrafficPermitted()` is consulted for them.
    This file therefore MUST NOT be presented as the security boundary for the
    hub's own protocol; it is not, and it never was.
  * Android's network-security-config supports HOSTNAMES and IP LITERALS only —
    there is no CIDR/subnet syntax — so a "just the LAN subnet" rule is not
    expressible. That is precisely why the posture is a permissive base-config
    rather than a scoped domain-config.

  PRODUCTION UPGRADE PATH (documented, deliberately NOT implemented here)
  =======================================================================
  1. Give every peer a certificate (or pin a self-signed CA per hub) and switch
     the gRPC transport to TLS; keep the hub listener on `TLS`-terminated sockets.
  2. Change `base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted` to `false` and scope any
     remaining cleartext to explicit `domain-config` entries.
  3. Re-test the Speechmatics client against `https://`/`wss://` only.

  Adding TLS is explicitly out of scope for T12 (the plan forbids it); this
  comment is the upgrade path, not a promise that it exists today.
  ============================================================================
-->
<network-security-config>
    <!-- DEV-ONLY: see the header. Cleartext stays permitted for the LAN until the
         TLS upgrade path above is executed. -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
